I'm very sorry this is so late in coming... I'm going out of town from 
July 13 - July 21. I will help you with your program, but it needs to wait 
until I get back.  If you want, I'll write the program for you and you can 
call it yours.  No big deal really, 'cause it sounds rather simple, and I 
don't mind.  It won't take alot of my time I don't think.  
 
If however, you need it right away, a couple words of advice:

there is no command as "assign" in standard pascal (atleast not on this compiler)
You do it all in reset and rewrite:   reset(garbin, 'filename'); 
(I think it's like that... not sure.. remember to include garbin in the 
files at the top:  program stuff (input, output, garbin);

Secondly, I think there is a string command in this version of pascal, but VCU
has it so poorly documented, I can't tell for sure.  You can implement your
own strings with this type:

type
   strings = record
        stringpart : packed array [1..maxchars] of char;
        lenpart : integer;
   end;

Define a constant called "maxchars" which is your maximum character length.
To address the elements of a record you use a period... Example:

var
   Astring: strings;
   charindex : integer;

begin
    for charindex := 1 to Astring.lenpart do
        write (Astring.stringpart[charindex]);
end.

That will (providing information has been loaded into the variable) print out
a string using the string type we defined.

Finally, the next piece of mail will contain a random number function.  I would
suggest removing the line that says something like: "seed := 47132" (or some
number like that).  This should give you more random results.  

Hope this helps.. if this isn't enough, then just mail me and I'll get back
to you on the 21st.
 
Macon




